# Copywriting Standards — The Editor

Read `00-brand-core.mdc` first. This file enforces the voice at the sentence level for client-facing and site copy.

Do not apply these sentence-level brand-copy constraints to `the-hard-port-os/**`. That repository uses its own OS rules in `05-hard-port-os.mdc`.

use ';" instead of "—" in files that are .tsx except for comments.  the reader cant be seeing this "—" being used. 

## Positioning anchor (SERVICE-001)

Public site copy describes **THP SMB service**, not the legacy media-agency package funnel.

| Say | Don't say (on public funnel) |
|---|---|
| Level 1 business, stability, constraint | "Content calendar," "rebrand," "ad-buying service" as the offer |
| Business Reality Diagnostic | "Marketing audit," "free strategy call," "social-media fix" |
| Application → qualification → diagnostic | "Free slot," "we pick 2 a month" (legacy — retired) |
| One active client (capacity) | Fake scarcity, countdown timers |
| THP decides acceptance | "We'd love to work with you" (audition dynamic reversed) |

Canonical commercial reference: `THP-SMB-SERVICE-001-offer-architecture.md`.

## Banned words and phrases

If any of these appear in new or edited copy, rewrite the sentence. They are the fingerprint
of generic agency/SaaS writing and instantly break the brand's credibility:

- "unlock," "leverage," "empower," "seamless," "game-changer," "solutions," "elevate your"
- "at your fingertips," "take your business to the next level," "we're passionate about"
- "detailed breakdown," "expand each feature," "compare features," "learn more" as a CTA
- "boost your business" (ironic — legacy boosting section is retired)
- "free slot," "apply free," "2 businesses a month" (legacy agency scarcity — unless Canon changes)
- Any sentence that describes the brand's own honesty or attitude out loud
  (e.g. "we'd rather tell you the truth than..." — just tell them, don't narrate it)

## Sentence rhythm rules

- Short sentences do the damage. Long sentences explain. Default to short.
- Second person, always. The visitor is "you," addressed directly, not "our clients" or "business
  owners."
- One idea per line where possible. Let white space do some of the punching.
- Punchlines land at the end of a section, not buried mid-paragraph.

## The escalation rule

Tone intensity is not flat across the page — it climbs. Early sections (hero, letter) can be dry
and observational. Mid-page (condition, mechanism, proof) get confrontational. Late sections
(boundaries, capacity, last call) carry the hardest lines. If a section near the bottom reads
softer than one near the top, that's a bug — flag it.

## Before / after (use this as the litmus test for new copy)

BAD (generic, explains itself):
> "We believe in transparency and always try to be upfront with our clients about what we can
> realistically deliver."

GOOD (Hard Port voice):
> "We don't have a hundred reviews. We're new. Take it or leave it."

BAD (legacy agency):
> "Choose the package that fits your growth stage — Port, Sea, or Ocean."

GOOD (SERVICE-001):
> "Application is free. The diagnostic is paid. Not everyone gets in."

## FAQ-specific rule

Every FAQ answer must match the aggression level of its question. If the question is blunt
("Why are you so rude?"), the answer cannot be a polite customer-service paragraph. Write the
answer as a comeback, not an explanation.

FAQ must address SERVICE-001 objections: marketing audit confusion, guarantees, acceptance,
diagnostic cost — not legacy free-slot or package questions.

## Service ladder / comparison rule

Never present Port/Sea/Ocean pricing grids or neutral SaaS tier comparisons on the public funnel.
If comparison UI is needed, it compares **business condition** (where you are vs where stability
requires), not feature checklists. Run `offer-construction` before adding any new commercial tier UI.
